   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Health Insurance Data Analysis & Insights\n",
    "**Portfolio project | Python · Pandas · Matplotlib · Seaborn**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Project goal\n",
    "Explore a public health-insurance-cost dataset to understand its structure, assess data quality, compare insurance charges across groups, and communicate patterns with visualizations.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Important limitation:** This is an observational dataset. Associations do not prove causation, and insurance charges are not the same as medical need or health outcomes. Do not use this analysis to make decisions about an individual.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Questions\n",
    "1. What does the dataset contain, and are there data-quality issues?\n",
    "2. How are insurance charges distributed?\n",
    "3. How do charges compare by smoking status?\n",
    "4. How are charges associated with age and BMI?\n",
    "5. Do the observed patterns vary across regions?\n",
    "\n",
    "## How to use this notebook\n",
    "Place the CSV file in `data/insurance.csv`, then run the notebook cells from top to bottom. The notebook checks the file path and prints results calculated from the data you actually load."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b73359b4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Import libraries and locate the dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "**In plain English:** libraries are collections of code other people have written. Pandas works with tables, Matplotlib and Seaborn make charts, and `Path` helps Python find files.\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook expects the CSV at `data/insurance.csv` in the project root. If your file has a different name, change `CSV_PATH` below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "df2ea1a5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "# Use a consistent visual style for charts\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\")\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", None)\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.float_format\", lambda value: f\"{value:,.2f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Find the project root whether the notebook runs from the root or notebooks/ folder\n",
    "cwd = Path.cwd()\n",
    "if (cwd / \"data\").is_dir():\n",
    "    PROJECT_DIR = cwd\n",
    "elif (cwd.parent / \"data\").is_dir():\n",
    "    PROJECT_DIR = cwd.parent\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    PROJECT_DIR = cwd\n",
    "\n",
    "CSV_PATH = PROJECT_DIR / \"data\" / \"insurance.csv\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Project folder:\", PROJECT_DIR.resolve())\n",
    "print(\"CSV path:\", CSV_PATH.resolve())\n",
    "print(\"File exists:\", CSV_PATH.exists())\n",
    "\n",
    "if not CSV_PATH.exists():\n",
    "    raise FileNotFoundError(\n",
    "        f\"Could not find {CSV_PATH}. Put your CSV in the project's data/ folder \"\n",
    "        \"and name it insurance.csv, or update CSV_PATH to your actual filename.\"\n",
    "    )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "77dd5e39",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Load the CSV\n",
    "\n",
    "**In plain English:** `read_csv` reads the file into a table called a DataFrame. `head()` shows the first five rows, and `shape` tells us the number of rows and columns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "98e86ed8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.read_csv(CSV_PATH)\n",
    "\n",
    print(f"Number of records: {df.shape[0]}")
    print(f"Number of columns: {df.shape[1]}")
    print(f"Duplicate rows: {df.duplicated().sum()}")
    "print(f\"Loaded {df.shape[0]:,} rows and {df.shape[1]} columns.\")\n",
    "display(df.head())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "377b95b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Check columns, data types, and basic statistics\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why this matters:** before analysis, we need to understand what each column contains. Summary statistics can reveal suspicious values, but they do not explain every issue by themselves."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "6154d54f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Column names:\")\n",
    "print(df.columns.tolist())\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nData types and non-null counts:\")\n",
    "df.info()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nNumeric summary:\")\n",
    "display(df.describe())\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nCategorical summary:\")\n",
    "display(df.describe(include=\"object\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fe11308d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Validate the expected schema\n",
    "\n",
    "The known dataset should include `age`, `sex`, `bmi`, `children`, `smoker`, `region`, and `charges`. This cell stops with a clear message if a required column is missing."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "bd13907f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "expected_columns = {\"age\", \"sex\", \"bmi\", \"children\", \"smoker\", \"region\", \"charges\"}\n",
    "missing_columns = expected_columns - set(df.columns)\n",
    "\n",
    "if missing_columns:\n",
    "    raise ValueError(f\"Required columns are missing: {sorted(missing_columns)}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"All expected columns are present.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "08f08502",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Data-quality checks\n",
    "\n",
    "We will count missing values and duplicate rows, inspect category labels, and flag implausible negative values. We will **not automatically delete rows**: cleaning choices should be justified by the data and project question."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2dc1feb6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Missing values per column:\")\n",
    "display(df.isna().sum().rename(\"missing_count\").to_frame())\n",
    "\n",
    "duplicate_count = int(df.duplicated().sum())\n",
    "print(f\"Exact duplicate rows: {duplicate_count:,}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "for column in [\"sex\", \"smoker\", \"region\"]:\n",
    "    print(f\"\\nValues in {column}:\")\n",
    "    display(df[column].value_counts(dropna=False).rename(\"count\").to_frame())\n",
    "\n",
    "nonnegative_columns = [\"age\", \"bmi\", \"children\", \"charges\"]\n",
    "print(\"\\nNegative values in fields expected to be non-negative:\")\n",
    "for column in nonnegative_columns:\n",
    "    negative_count = int((pd.to_numeric(df[column], errors=\"coerce\") < 0).sum())\n",
    "    print(f\"{column}: {negative_count}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1c6894e2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Optional: Create a working copy\n",
    "\n",
    "We preserve `df` as the original loaded data and create `analysis_df` for analysis. Exact duplicate rows are reported but not automatically removed because repeated-looking records may need investigation before deletion."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "33176b8e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "analysis_df = df.copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Standardize whitespace in categorical text without changing the original df.\n",
    "for column in [\"sex\", \"smoker\", \"region\"]:\n",
    "    analysis_df[column] = analysis_df[column].astype(\"string\").str.strip().str.lower()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Working copy created:\", analysis_df.shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b2f48077",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Check distributions and unusual values\n",
    "\n",
    "Histograms help us see the shape of numeric variables. The boxplots show potential outliers; an outlier is not automatically an error."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "8c6543b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "numeric_columns = [\"age\", \"bmi\", \"children\", \"charges\"]\n",
    "analysis_df[numeric_columns].hist(figsize=(12, 8), bins=20, edgecolor=\"black\")\n",
    "plt.suptitle(\"Distributions of Numeric Variables\", y=1.02)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9391dd38",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.boxplot(data=analysis_df, x=\"smoker\", y=\"charges\", ax=axes[0])\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Charges by Smoking Status\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"Smoker\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Insurance charges\")\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.boxplot(data=analysis_df, x=\"region\", y=\"charges\", ax=axes[1])\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"Charges by Region\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"Region\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"Insurance charges\")\n",
    "axes[1].tick_params(axis=\"x\", rotation=20)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "581e19a6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Research question: how do charges compare by smoking status?\n",
    "\n",
    "We calculate group size, mean, median, and standard deviation. The mean is sensitive to very large charges; the median describes the middle record in each group."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e0dd99c4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "smoking_summary = (\n",
    "    analysis_df.groupby(\"smoker\", dropna=False)[\"charges\"]\n",
    "    .agg(count=\"count\", mean=\"mean\", median=\"median\", std=\"std\")\n",
    "    .sort_index()\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(smoking_summary.round(2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a496ad02",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "sns.boxplot(data=analysis_df, x=\"smoker\", y=\"charges\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Insurance Charges by Smoking Status\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Smoker\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Insurance charges\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "19612160",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation prompt:** Based on your table and boxplot, which group has the higher median and mean charges? How much do the distributions overlap? Describe only what your results show; this comparison does not prove that smoking alone caused a charge difference."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c595f6f4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Research question: how are charges associated with age and BMI?\n",
    "\n",
    "Scatterplots show how two numeric variables move together. The trend line is a descriptive summary, not proof of cause."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "49400e84",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.regplot(\n",
    "    data=analysis_df, x=\"age\", y=\"charges\",\n",
    "    scatter_kws={\"alpha\": 0.35}, line_kws={\"color\": \"red\"}, ax=axes[0]\n",
    ")\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Insurance Charges vs Age\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"Age\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Insurance charges\")\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.regplot(\n",
    "    data=analysis_df, x=\"bmi\", y=\"charges\",\n",
    "    scatter_kws={\"alpha\": 0.35}, line_kws={\"color\": \"red\"}, ax=axes[1]\n",
    ")\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"Insurance Charges vs BMI\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"BMI\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"Insurance charges\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fdbeaa93",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "correlations = analysis_df[[\"age\", \"bmi\", \"children\", \"charges\"]].corr(numeric_only=True)\n",
    "display(correlations.round(3))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(correlations, annot=True, cmap=\"vlag\", center=0, fmt=\".2f\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Correlation Matrix for Numeric Variables\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "203e515d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation prompt:** Correlation ranges from -1 to +1. Values near +1 indicate a positive linear association, values near -1 indicate a negative linear association, and values near 0 indicate little linear association. Correlation does not establish causation and may miss nonlinear patterns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "76a9ffb7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Research question: how do charges vary by region?\n",
    "\n",
    "We summarize charges by region, then visualize the median and mean. Differences may reflect several factors not measured in this dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "c19d9daa",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "region_summary = (\n",
    "    analysis_df.groupby(\"region\")[\"charges\"]\n",
    "    .agg(count=\"count\", mean=\"mean\", median=\"median\")\n",
    "    .sort_values(\"median\", ascending=False)\n",
    ")\n",
    "display(region_summary.round(2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "0cd2cd53",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(9, 5))\n",
    "sns.barplot(data=analysis_df, x=\"region\", y=\"charges\", estimator=\"median\", errorbar=None)\n",
    "plt.title(\"Median Insurance Charges by Region\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Region\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Median insurance charges\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0d56af49",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 10. Generate a results-based summary\n",
    "\n",
    "This cell calculates summary statements from the loaded data. It does not assume particular values. Review the outputs before using any statement in a report."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "5f803626",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"DATASET SUMMARY\")\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(f\"Records: {len(analysis_df):,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Columns: {analysis_df.shape[1]}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Missing cells: {int(analysis_df.isna().sum().sum()):,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Exact duplicate rows: {int(analysis_df.duplicated().sum()):,}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nOVERALL CHARGES\")\n",
    "print(f\"Mean: {analysis_df['charges'].mean():,.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Median: {analysis_df['charges'].median():,.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Minimum: {analysis_df['charges'].min():,.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Maximum: {analysis_df['charges'].max():,.2f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nSMOKING STATUS GROUPS\")\n",
    "display(smoking_summary.round(2))\n",
    "\n",
    "if analysis_df[\"smoker\"].nunique(dropna=True) >= 2:\n",
    "    medians = analysis_df.groupby(\"smoker\")[\"charges\"].median().dropna()\n",
    "    if len(medians) >= 2:\n",
    "        highest_group = medians.idxmax()\n",
    "        lowest_group = medians.idxmin()\n",
    "        print(\n",
    "            f\"Highest median charges among smoking-status groups: {highest_group} \"\n",
    "            f\"({medians.max():,.2f})\"\n",
    "        )\n",
    "        print(\n",
    "            f\"Lowest median charges among smoking-status groups: {lowest_group} \"\n",
    "            f\"({medians.min():,.2f})\"\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nREGION SUMMARY\")\n",
    "display(region_summary.round(2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "813e1699",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 11. Conclusions and limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "After running all cells, write 3–5 findings in your own words based on the actual outputs. Useful prompts:\n",
    "\n",
    "- What did the data-quality checks reveal?\n",
    "- Which smoking-status group had higher typical charges?\n",
    "- How did charges appear to relate to age and BMI?\n",
    "- Did median charges differ by region?\n",
    "- What limitations mean we should be cautious?\n",
    "\n",
    "### Limitations\n",
    "- This is observational data, so patterns do not prove cause and effect.\n",
    "- The dataset contains insurance charges, not a complete picture of health.\n",
    "- Results may not generalize to other insurers, locations, or time periods.\n",
    "- Unmeasured variables may explain some observed differences.\n",
    "- Do not use this analysis to make medical, insurance eligibility, or individual pricing decisions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8dfeb4f6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 12. Export a small summary table\n",
    "\n",
    "This creates a CSV of group summaries that can be included with the project. The exported file contains calculated aggregates, not the original row-level dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3196b10f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "output_dir = PROJECT_DIR / \"outputs\"\n",
    "output_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "summary_path = output_dir / \"charges_by_smoking_status.csv\"\n",
    "smoking_summary.to_csv(summary_path)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Saved summary to:\", summary_path)"
   ]
  },
